Jove wrote:
> On 8 Feb 2006 11:44:00 -0800, "sjdevnull@yahoo.com" wrote:
> >The problem here is that simple damage just isn't a deterrent to people
> >who want to power up forever.
>
> >Altar camp until you have a bazillion HP.
>
>   I think there's pretty much one reason to get a bazillion hit
> points.  It's so the player can focus on the real threat:
> instadeaths.

Sure.  My idea is to cut out the "basically impossible to die" portions
of Nethack (endless mazes in Gehennom with no real threats to most
characters) and streamline the late game to where it's fun and
interesting to play.

I'm not interested in making the game insanely vicious, just in making
the part between the Castle and the Sanctum about as risky (and
interesting) as the preceding part.

> NB: You can die with a bazillion hit points.

Not after the Castle, until Rodney and the Planes, unless you either
run into Demogorgon or get really careless.  I mean, I've died maybe
three times after the castle, once to Rodney, once on the Planes, and
once picking up a 'trice corpse barehanded.  That's even after cutting
down to under 50,000 turns and 150 HP on a few of my last ascensions.

Indeed, I've considered the "get to the Castle and quit" approach since
there's a fairly long boring trudge through Gehennom before you finally
start the (once again interesting) Ascension run at that point.

Evening out that mid-game is a worthy goal, I think, and a _lot_ of
other people seem to feel the same way.

> >That said, a few more rare hard-hitting monsters that a skilled player
> >could avoid or deal with from afar make sense as a deterrent to someone
> >blindly doing Valk Smash!  Make some monsters that are immune to
> >physical weapons, so there's a reason to actually use up attack wands
> >in combat (and to save some in the mid-game).
>
>   Punishing less-skilled players.

That's a good thing.  A system that rewards more-skilled players and
punishes less-skilled ones is a fair one, and most likely to result in
interesting gameplay IMO.

> And making "some monsters that are immune to physical weapons" is just
> turning Gehennom into a puzzle game. Memorize the solution to the puzzle
> and it becomes boring.

Not really.  Unlike, say, Medusa or Juiblex, it would not be a case of
"there's one password that kills this beastie".  It'd be a case of
"there's one thing that doesn't work here, but you can use any other
solution to get around it".

It would, in fact, increase the prominence of spellcasters (who you're
keen on as differentiating gameplay).  But you wouldn't have to be a
spellcaster; you could use those wands you've stockpiled (currently
pretty much useless except as polyfodder in the mid-late game), get
them in the line of fire of dragons breath, run around them, lock them
in rooms, whatever.

>   Right now, Juiblex is like that.  I've seen monsters like this
> referred to as "October monsters."  They're almost invulnerable
> until you say "October" to them.  Then they fall over dead.

Those are crappy, hence my suggestion to at least not make Juiblex
basically die from a wand of digging.  At least Medusa has a modicum of
historical interest going for her, but really after the first time you
meet her she's a bore.

> >> * Remove the ability to unrestrict weapon skills. It severely breaks
> >> roleplaying and character differentiation. Also, your god should only
> >> give you a weapon artifact if you can already reach "skilled" in its
> >> weapon skill.
> >
> >Yes.  Also forbid wishing for Quest artifacts.
>
>   A healer could have the +7 Mitre of Holiness, the Eye of
> Aethopica, a robe, and one thousand mana points, and the healer
> still couldn't advance spell skills in any school except healing.

Sure, but he'd have astral vision, which is a pretty unique monk trait
otherwise.

>   Any non-Valkyrie could have gauntlets of power and Mjollnir,
> but Mjollnir still wouldn't return to their hand when thrown.

Mjollnir's not a quest artifact.  But increasing the minor differences
like this would be nice as well.

>   A non-knight with the Magic Mirror of Merlin wouldn't get
> double damage to the magic missile spell.

Sure, but they'd get magic resistance and ESP "for free".

It's not a major thing, but e.g. limiting the Eye to wizards makes them
better spellcasters than everyone (as it should be--they're _wizards_).
 Ditto the unique properties of the Eyes, the Staff of Aesclepius,
Platinum Yendorian Express Card, etc; for those artifacts that have
somewhat unique properties, limiting them to their class increases
differentiation.

>   Spell casting abilities differentiate characters, if you let
> them.  If you won't let spell casting make a difference, nothing
> can force you to differentiate characters.

Obviously there are _other_ distinctions between classes, like which
spells they can cast, but even more variety is a good thing.

> >Make unique monsters tougher.  Demogorgon and Rodney are threats, as
> >are the riders.  Everything else is pretty laughable.  Especially make
> >Vlad a threat, but also Juiblex (digging only gets you out of him and
> >damages him some, but not to 1HP), Orcus (maybe his /Death is an
> >artifact wand that has a chance against reflection/MR), etc.  Perhaps
> >make =FreeAction less potent vs. paralyzation, at least paralyzation
> >from uniques and the high priest of Moloch--still work against other
> >priests, gel cubes, etc.
>
>   There's a bigger problem here than I think you realize.  Right
> now a skilled player who plays carefully can ascend without great
> weapons, AC, etc.  E.g.: speed ascenders.

Sure, but it's a massive problem when archons, liches, and other
"common-ish" monsters are _far_ more of a threat than the majority of
unique baddies.

> >Up the HP, lower the AC, and make them have some combat/spell threat to
> >a kitted-out player.  Also, make most of them _not_ do the "teleport to
> >the stairs" bit; that's interesting at first, but when everything is
> >doing it then you know how to deal with it and you're just doing the
> >same (boring) motions repeatedly.
>
>   And punish both careful skilled players, and semi-skilled
> players that don't want to "altar camp until they have a
> bazillion hit points."

No, the point of combining this with a hp limit is to make these
combats less mechanical and make them interesting challenges without
one preferred solution.

I mean, in the earlier game a mumak or a horde of ants requires some
thought, but it's hardly punitive on a skilled player (even a
speed-ascender).  It's a lot more fun to play than the tedium of
Gehennom wherein a careful player is basically invulnerable even in the
face of demon lords, and making Gehennom shorter and a bit tougher
seems likely to make the game more interesting for both the
combat-wombats and the Rasts of the world.

>   Right now your proposals seem determined to punish everyone.

The thread is "thoughts on making Nethack harder", so you'd expect to
see suggestions that make nethack harder.  Admittedly some of my
suggestions (shorten Gehennom, allow teleporting with the amulet)
actually make it easier, but I think the elimination of repetitive
action warrants that.

>   You seem to have described Slash'Em almost completely.

How's that?  Slash'em is even more geared to "one problem, one
solution" than Nethack, has more "checklist" items that you need to kit
up with, and is harder in the early game.  I'm talking about things to
eliminate "you have to do this stupid pet trick" one-solution enemies,
reducing the number of "must-have" ascension kit items, and making the
late game on a par with the early-mid game and the late-game but not
harder.

And I'm not talking about making the game longer with more branches,
I'm talking about shortening it up where it gets boring and making for
more fun play.



